  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "If \\(y=x+\\dfrac1x,\\) then \\(x^4+x^3-4x^2+x+1=0\\) becomes:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-2)=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-3)=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-4)=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(x^2(y^2+y-6)=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "none of these"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1953-p20.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Because \\(x\\ne0,\\) factor the expression as \\[ \\begin{aligned} x^2\\Bigg[& \\left(x^2+\\frac1{x^2}\\right)\\\\ &+\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)-4 \\Bigg]. \\end{aligned} \\] Since \\(x^2+x^{-2}=y^2-2,\\) this becomes \\[ x^2(y^2+y-6)=0. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Factor out \\(x^2\\) and group reciprocal terms",
        "Use \\(x^2+x^{-2}=(x+x^{-1})^2-2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
